.TH "mbedded\-cbor" "3" "2026-10-31" "mbedded\-cbor 0.9.0" "mbedded\-cbor"
.SH NAME
mbedded\-cbor \- a header\-only C++ library for CBOR (RFC 8949)
.SH DESCRIPTION
mbedded\-cbor is a header\-only C++ library for CBOR (RFC 8949).
Its goal: a read touches only the bytes that it needs, and copies none
of them.
A read goes to one value and skips every other item by its length.
A text comes back as a view into the message.
A struct that you describe is written as Packed CBOR, so a read of a
field is one jump to an offset that the compiler knows.
Every message that the library writes is standard CBOR, and every other
CBOR decoder reads it.
Bad input comes back as a \f[CR]cbor::error\f[R] value, and no read goes
outside the message.
.SS lazy
Use it to walk a message whose shape you learn while you read it: a map,
then a key, then an element.
Cost: a step by key or index reads the heads up to the child, O(bytes
before the child).
No allocation.
A text is a view into the message.
.SS path
Use it to read one value when you know its path, for example
\f[CR]$.user.id\f[R].
The path is checked at compile time.
Cost: O(bytes up to the value).
No allocation for a number, a \f[CR]bool\f[R] or a view.
A path that selects many nodes allocates its list.
.SS item
Use it when you need the whole message as a tree that lives on after the
bytes are gone, or to write CBOR item by item with the encoder.
Cost: \f[CR]decode\f[R] is O(bytes) and copies every string into one
memory zone.
The encoder is O(1) per head and allocates nothing over a
\f[CR]std::span<char>\f[R].
.SS structs
Use it to read CBOR that any program wrote into the members of a struct,
and to write it back.
Cost: O(bytes) for a read and a write.
The result allocates its strings and containers.
.SS schema
Use it for messages that your own programs write and read.
Cost: \f[CR]path\f[R] opens a message in O(1) of its size, and a read of
a field of fixed size is one jump that checks only the bytes it reads.
\f[CR]at_path\f[R] with no accessor allocates nothing.
\f[CR]encode\f[R] is O(bytes), and a list of fixed\-size structs is one
copy.
.SS ref
Use it to keep a result that points into the message valid: it holds the
owner of the bytes.
Cost: O(1) for each access; a copy changes one atomic count.
\f[CR]clone\f[R] copies, O(bytes).
.SS tools
Use \f[CR]item_size\f[R] to find where an item ends, \f[CR]sequence\f[R]
and \f[CR]sequence_reader\f[R] to read many items one after the other,
and \f[CR]diagnostic_notation\f[R] to print an item as text.
Cost: \f[CR]item_size\f[R] is O(items) with no allocation.
\f[CR]sequence_reader\f[R] keeps only the bytes not yet read.
\f[CR]diagnostic_notation\f[R] is O(bytes) and allocates its text.
.SS errors
Use it to read why a call failed: each failure is one
\f[CR]cbor::error\f[R], and it converts to \f[CR]std::error_code\f[R].
Cost: none; an error is a small value.
.SH SEE ALSO
cbor::lazy(3), cbor::at_path(3), cbor::item(3), cbor::structs(3), cbor::schema(3), cbor::ref(3), cbor::item_size(3), cbor::sequence(3), cbor::sequence_reader(3), cbor::diagnostic_notation(3), cbor::error(3)
